course: world
lesson: 90
title: Measuring the world — length, mass, time and temperature
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Long ago people measured with hands, feet and steps, but everyone's feet are different, so the same room could be "ten feet" for one person and "twelve" for another.
explain: So people agreed on standard units that are the same for everyone: the metre for length, the kilogram for mass, the second for time and the degree Celsius for temperature.
explain: We choose a tool that fits the job: a ruler or tape measure for length, scales for mass, a clock or stopwatch for time and a thermometer for temperature.
explain: Mass is how much stuff is in something; weight is how hard gravity pulls on it. On the Moon an astronaut's mass stays the same but his weight is about six times smaller.
explain: Example: a 1-litre bottle of water has a mass of about 1 kilogram, so you can feel what a kilogram is just by lifting it.
fact: A standard unit is a measure that is the same for everyone.
fact: A metre is 100 centimetres, and a kilometre is 1000 metres.
fact: A kilogram is 1000 grams.
fact: One litre of water has a mass of about one kilogram.
fact: A minute is 60 seconds and an hour is 60 minutes.
fact: Water freezes at 0 degrees Celsius and boils at about 100 degrees Celsius.
fact: The normal temperature inside a healthy human body is about 37 degrees Celsius; a temperature well above that, such as 39, is a fever, a sign of illness.
fact: Mass is the amount of matter in an object; weight is the pull of gravity on it.
fact: An object has the same mass on the Moon as on Earth, but it weighs less there.
fact: When measuring with a ruler you must start from the zero mark.
fact: Measuring the same thing several times and taking the average (add the results up and divide by how many there are) gives a more reliable result.
exercise: Why did people stop measuring with their feet? | Different people's feet are different sizes | Feet are too short to measure anything | It was too tiring to walk | Shoes made it impossible
exercise: Which tool would you use to measure your height? | A tape measure | A thermometer | A stopwatch | Kitchen scales
exercise: Which tool measures temperature? | A thermometer | A ruler | A magnetic compass | A stopwatch
exercise: How many centimetres are in a metre? | 100 | 10 | 1000 | 60
exercise: How many metres are in a kilometre? | 1000 | 100 | 10 | 10000
exercise: How many grams are in a kilogram? | 1000 | 100 | 10 | 60
exercise: About what mass does one litre of water have? | One kilogram | One gram | Ten kilograms | One hundred grams
exercise: At what temperature does pure water freeze? | 0 degrees Celsius | 10 degrees Celsius | 100 degrees Celsius | 37 degrees Celsius
exercise: About what is the normal temperature inside your body? | 37 degrees Celsius | 0 degrees Celsius | 20 degrees Celsius | 100 degrees Celsius
exercise: What is mass? | The amount of matter in an object | The pull of gravity on an object | The size of the space an object takes up | How fast an object falls
exercise: What is weight? | The pull of gravity on an object | The amount of matter in an object | The length of an object | How hot an object is
exercise: Where must you start when measuring with a ruler? | At the zero mark | At the end of the ruler | At the number one | Anywhere you like
exercise: How many seconds are in a minute? | 60 | 100 | 10 | 24
exercise: Why do scientists measure the same thing several times? | To get a more reliable result | To make the number bigger | Because the first try never counts | To use up the time
test: An astronaut with a mass of 60 kilograms goes to the Moon. What is his mass there? | Still 60 kilograms | About 10 kilograms | Nothing at all | About 360 kilograms
test: Which is true? | A kilometre is longer than a thousand centimetres | A kilogram is less than a gram | Water freezes at 100 degrees Celsius | A minute is 100 seconds
test: Sam and his dad each measure the garden in steps and get different numbers. What would solve the problem? | Using a tape measure in metres | Taking much smaller steps each time | Walking faster | Measuring at night
test: Ella measures a pencil starting from the 1 cm mark and reads 13 cm. How long is it really? | 12 cm | 13 cm | 14 cm | 1 cm
test: Which pair matches the right tool? | Time and a stopwatch | Mass and a thermometer | Temperature and a ruler | Length and kitchen scales
test: A full 2-litre bottle of water is put on scales. About what will they show, not counting the bottle? | 2 kilograms | 2 grams | 20 kilograms | 200 kilograms
test: A child's thermometer shows 39 degrees Celsius. What does it suggest? | The child has a fever | The child's body is too cold | The temperature is normal | Water would freeze on the child's skin
test: Three pupils time the same toy car run: 4.1, 4.3 and 4.2 seconds. What is the best value to report? | The average, about 4.2 seconds | Only the fastest, 4.1 seconds | Only the slowest, 4.3 seconds | The total, 12.6 seconds
test: Why do shops all use the same kilogram? | So that everyone gets the same amount for the same price | So that bags break less often | So that food stays fresh longer | So that scales need no batteries
test: how many grams in a kilo again | 1000 | 100 | 10 | 10,000
test: measured the table 3 times and got 3 diff numbers, what now | Take the average of the measurements | Use only the biggest number, because it's safest | Use only the first number | Throw them all away and guess
test: Leo says u'd have less mass on the Moon. true? | No, mass stays the same, but you weigh less there | Yes, the Moon takes away some of your mass | Yes, and your weight stays the same | No, you weigh more on the Moon
test: my brother started measuring from the end of the ruler, not the zero. is that right? | No, you must start from the zero mark | Yes, any end of the ruler works | Yes, if you add one centimetre | No, you must start from the 1 mark
exam: Ascertain the number of grams contained in one kilogram. | One thousand | One hundred | Ten | Ten thousand
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between mass and weight? | Mass is the amount of matter; weight is the pull of gravity upon it | Mass is the pull of gravity; weight is the amount of matter | Mass and weight are identical in every place | Mass changes upon the Moon; weight does not
exam: It is given that an object is transported from the Earth to the Moon. Determine which of its quantities remains unaltered. | Its mass | Its weight | Both its mass and its weight | Neither its mass nor its weight
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the obtaining of a reliable measurement. | Measure several times and take the average | Measure once only and with great speed | Measure from any mark upon the ruler | Choose the largest of several measurements
